fact WorkItem(id: "WI-651", description: some(value: "neq(map, map) SILENT-MISDECIDE hazard escapes the WI-650 load check. check_eq_override_backing catches eq(map,map) because the typer stamps the eq operand's inferred_type as the concrete carrier Map; but for a neq(?a,?b) rule-body goal over two Maps the typer UNDER-DETERMINES each operand's inferred_type to the ABSTRACT param anthill.prelude.Map.K (neq's dictionary threading picks up Map's 'requires Eq[T=K]' rather than concretizing the receiver to Map, unlike eq which is a direct SemEq builtin). So operand_unbacked_eq_carrier reads Map.K (a sort-param symbol, correctly declined), the check does NOT fire, and neq(map1,map2) — which resolves as not(eq(map1,map2)) over the empty Map.eq override — silently returns the WRONG Bool at runtime (equal maps read as neq=true). This is the same silent-misdecide class WI-650 exists to prevent, for neq instead of eq; narrow (only Map, only neq, only unbacked-override carriers). ROOT CAUSE is the typer's neq operand-type inference (likely the neq(?a,?b) <=> not(eq(?a,?b)) unfold interacting with Map's requires), NOT the WI-650 check itself (which already matches Eq.neq via is_eq_call and would fire if the operand concretized). FIX DIRECTIONS: (a) make the typer concretize a neq operand to its receiver carrier the way eq does (preferred — closes it at the source, and would let map_eq_error_test.rs add a neq-in-rule-body case, currently omitted with a NOTE); (b) as a fallback, have check_eq_override_backing recognize a neq/eq operand typed as a carrier type-PARAM and probe the enclosing dictionary — brittle, rejected unless (a) is infeasible. Acceptance: neq(map,map) in a rule/op body fails to load with the EqOverrideUnbacked diagnostic (mirror the eq tests); full suite green. Follow-up to WI-650."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T11:43:50Z"))
